 this city of  seven million people would help make the  rest of China more open and free do you  think China is becoming more like Hong  Kong or as Hong Kong becoming more like  China this general sense that China will  slowly democratize in Hong Kong would  benefit from that seems to be a pipe  dream and in the past you know five or  six years we have seen really an  acceleration of China's political agenda  on Hong Kong Beijing is tightening its  grip it recently blocked  pro-independence lawmakers from taking  office and abducted five publishers who  sold books critical of the Chinese  government Lam Winkie was detained for  eight months and says he was forced to  sign a confession he says if the Chinese  government does not follow Hong Kong's  Constitution the city will have no  choice but to push for independence most  people here don't believe China would  ever allow Hong Kong to be truly  independent but protests like this have  soared more than 200 percent in the past  decade because people here fear their  way of life is  ataque 20 year old activist Joshua Wong  was arrested this week at a protest he  led opposition to so-called patriotic  education in Hong Kong schools and was  part of the massive protests that shut  down large parts of the city in 2014  this 20th anniversary is being called a  celebration by some how does it feel to  you it's not the time for celebration  it's time for demonstration they have  determined to be one country one system  we hope to maintain democracy and ask  for autonomy but in his speech before he  left Hong Kong Chinese President Xi  Jingping gave a pretty stern warning  he said that any challenge to China's  sovereignty or the central government's  authority crosses a red line and won't  be tolerated Anthony and Tracy in Hong  Kong thanks Ben

